  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Hawaii received $2.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government recorded 25,568 awards in the state with an average award size of $114K. Obligations fell 47.3% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a rise from $2.8B in FY21 to a peak of $6.7B in FY24, then a drop to $5.5B in FY25 and $2.9B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $2.2B, followed by the General Services Administration with $401M and the Department of Homeland Security with $128M. Construction and engineering dominate the industry picture. Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounted for $603M, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $553M, and Engineering Services accounted for $390M. The largest vendors reflect that profile. NAN INC received $388M, DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV received $353M, and LEIDOS, INC. received $121M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top federal buyers in Hawaii?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.2B in Hawaii in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $401M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $128M. Smaller buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs with $52M and the Department of the Interior with $42.8M. Several civilian agencies made more modest obligations, including the Department of Commerce with $25.3M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $5.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the most federal dollars in Hawaii?",
        "body": "Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounted for $603M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $553M. Engineering Services captured $390M and Computer Systems Design Services captured $186M. NAN INC received $388M and DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV received $353M, followed by LEIDOS, INC. at $121M and CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC at $97.3M."
      }
    ]
  },
